"Declared plugin signal latency and measured processing wall time describe different things. Wall time includes scheduling and preemption; it is not added to signal delay or equivalent to CPU utilization. Over-budget blocks and late wakes are clues, not proof of an audible glitch. The helper waits for a signaled full block rather than repeatedly sleeping. Wake delay measures notification to worker resume, excluding legitimate idle time. A Mach real-time policy is requested on its dedicated thread; the result below is the setup result, not a guarantee or continuous policy audit. The asynchronous worker uses its own audio workgroup and passes its context to supporting plugins. Interval deadlines describe block throughput; they do not promise device deadlines.\n\nWith 32-frame blocks at 48 kHz, one block is only 0.67 ms. Whole-chain bypass still renders plugins. Switching processing off retains the helper and fades to dry audio before suspending plugin rendering. A start with processing off loads no plugins; enabling loads the saved chain live without restarting the devices. Reset counters before comparisons. Startup filling is expected; subsequent lost frames remain visible even if they are difficult to hear. Per-plugin wall timings are for the current plugin in that slot, including preemption. They are not reset by Reset counters. A plugin's late count compares its own render to the whole block budget; several individually fast plugins can still exceed that budget together. Starvation captures the first short callback, not subsequent refill silence. Queue/stage fields are approximate concurrent samples; an active slot or a previous slow wake is not proof of causality." = "Declared plugin signal latency and measured processing wall time describe different things. Wall time includes scheduling and preemption; it is not added to signal delay or equivalent to CPU utilization. Over-budget blocks and late wakes are clues, not proof of an audible glitch. The helper waits for a signaled full block rather than repeatedly sleeping. Wake delay measures notification to worker resume, excluding legitimate idle time. A Mach real-time policy is requested on its dedicated thread; the result below is the setup result, not a guarantee or continuous policy audit. The asynchronous worker uses its own audio workgroup and passes its context to supporting plugins. Interval deadlines describe block throughput; they do not promise device deadlines.\n\nWith 32-frame blocks at 48 kHz, one block is only 0.67 ms. Whole-chain bypass still renders plugins. Switching processing off retains the helper and fades to dry audio before suspending plugin rendering. A start with processing off loads no plugins; enabling loads the saved chain live without restarting the devices. Reset counters before comparisons. Startup filling is expected; subsequent lost frames remain visible even if they are difficult to hear. Per-plugin wall timings are for the current plugin in that slot, including preemption. They are not reset by Reset counters. A plugin's late count compares its own render to the whole block budget; several individually fast plugins can still exceed that budget together. Starvation captures the first short callback, not subsequent refill silence. Queue/stage fields are approximate concurrent samples; an active slot or a previous slow wake is not proof of causality.";
